Z||000006||falconhulk||08-27-2001||09:19 AM||||Man these "pit" manuvers that the cops do really Pi$$'s me off.  I dont understand why they dont just have a copter follow them until they run out of gas.  There is no reason to ruin a car and casue damage to others property.  Some of those cars are new but most are old and will not be relplaced by insurance.  I just dont get this.  And afterwards they all walk around and say "damn that was cool"  even though someones hardworl os down the drain!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/i-4_b_l.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Formula88||08-27-2001||10:32 AM||||Well, the rationale is that they have to stop the chase, because the longer someone's trying to evade the Police, the more danger there is of an innocent bystander getting injured.  People will even try to outrun the Police Helicopters, so that won't even work. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Ryan Wright||08-27-2001||11:24 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>I dont understand why they dont just have a copter follow them until they run out of gas. <HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>OK, then we can increase YOUR taxes by 150% to pay for the $1000+ per hour it costs to operate that chopper. Considering most vehicles can probably go at least 5 hours, this could get spendy in a hurry.<P>Personally, I vote for bringing in a military chopper and launching a laser guided missile at these people. Would save a lot of taxpayer money by keeping them out of that luxury resort we like to call prison, not to mention court costs, etc... <P>In all honesty, when someone is running from the cops, they have every right to destroy the vehicle if that's what it takes to get it stopped. Otherwise, the suspects are likely to kill someone else in the process of trying to get away. Sucks when it's a Fiero, but, better the Fiero than an innocent bystander.<BR><P>------------------<BR>-Ryan Wright (http://www.ryanwright.com)||||reg||1
Z||000009||falconhulk||08-27-2001||11:32 AM||||who do you think pays to fix the police cars?  Ou tax money.  Who pays for the totaled car?  We do by paying ever increasing insurance rates.  They dont care about the destruction becasue its not thier property. A lot of these guys think its "cool" just like a kid does when watches it on TV.  Many cities have no chase policies in place.  They endanger people on the road when chasing, they endanger themselves.  I agree if its a murder then they should chase.  A stolen car is a crime usually done by a young person.  They usually do not have very good judgement or driving ability under normal circumstances.  By putting the pressure on them like that you endanger many lives and much property.  If the guy is driving like he wants to kill someone then hey do a "pick"  but most of the time they just do it becasue he will not stop.  Think about it.  If your Fiero was stolen and the police "picked" it into a ditch totaling it how would you feel?  Suppose it was a car even more rare then the Fiero?  Its not worth it.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/i-4_b_l.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||FieroLisa||08-27-2001||01:07 PM||||Personally, I'm a fan of doing whatever possible to stop a chase, even if it is a Fiero. My life is more important that that cars tires or fender.  And anyway who's to say that runaway vehicle isn't going to run a red light straight into *your* precious fiero? Not like car thiefs are courteous drivers or anything.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/fierolisa.gif">    <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>FieroLisa's Website</A>*<A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ncfieros/" TARGET=_blank>NC Tarheels Fieros Site</A><P>AIM: FieroLisa||||reg||1
Z||000011||falconhulk||08-27-2001||02:41 PM||||Thats exactly right.  They dont care about the car becasue they dont own it.  The cops dont care because they dont own it.  So if you have that situation then somethings going to get f%$$ed up!  They smash up the car without pressure from the cops.  When they are in a all out haul to get away the care even less.  I dont know the soloution but there has to be a better way.  ||||reg||1
Z||000012||JSocha||08-27-2001||02:54 PM||||Many states ARE enacting chase policies. It has been shown that it is more dangerous to try and pursue the car thief, especially in cities, as both the thief as well as the officers following are raising the stakes by forcing the criminal to flee even harder to avoid capture.<P>Much like a cat running away from a dog which is in pursuit.<P>They have found, especially in cities where helicopters are used, that the thief will eventually slow down and act more responsibly, even if he encounters police that do not make an immidate attempt to pursue, but later turn around and follow the thief from a distance having all traffic information relayed from the copter.  In most cases, they are able to apprehend the thief after a short while as he becomes complacent about the thought of not being captured or pursued.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||falconhulk||08-27-2001||02:57 PM||||Hey what do you know.  Thats exactly what I was thinking.  I would hope thats the way things go in the future<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/i-4_b_l.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||isthiswhereiputausername?||08-27-2001||02:57 PM||||there was another one on there a few weeks back.. dark red notchback ..<BR>cops had stopped the driver and he was walking a straight line when a package of drugs fell out of his shorts.. he was arrested..||||reg||1
Z||000015||Cheever3000||08-27-2001||03:05 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>it was a RED 88 GT T-TOP.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE>All they had ta do was get on a twisty-turny road.  No contest.  Except in the intelligence department.  I wouldn't mind taking a few swats at em.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||ChopTop||08-27-2001||04:11 PM||||I'm extremly ambivalent to police pursuits. Generally speaking, I don't agree with police pursuits.  Chasing someone down because they have a burned out taillight isn't an acceptable reason to put my life, or the life of my family or friends lifes at risk.  Add in a highly populated area and it makes it even less sense.  Also, who is more cognizant of the fact that police pursuits can/do end violently?  I'm betting it's the police since they deal with the aftermath of traffic deaths regularly.  Having that knowledge carries the additional responsibility of having to know when to pursue and when to let it go.<BR>Having said that...I also believe there are instances where the police have the duty to pursue.  Mainly when someone's driving behavior is putting the publics health at risk (and I don't mean because they are being chased).  After that the slippery slope comes in, when is it appropriate?  And who decides when it is?  The police officer, the community, the politicians?  I certainly don't have the answer.<BR>So if you do get stopped by the police don't be a jerk, pull over, comply with the police officers requests, take your ticket and go on your way.  The worst that can happen is you go to jail for a night, you will survive, your parents/wife/husband will get over it...eventually.  And the best part is you'll be around to go to all the great Fiero events!||||reg||1
Z||000017||My7Fieros||08-27-2001||04:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by falconhulk:<BR><B>Thats exactly right.  They dont care about the car becasue they dont own it.  The cops dont care because they dont own it.  So if you have that situation then somethings going to get f%$$ed up!  They smash up the car without pressure from the cops.  When they are in a all out haul to get away the care even less.  I dont know the soloution but there has to be a better way.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B><I>My 7 Fieros</FONT></FONT></B></I>||||reg||8
Z||000018||Urchin||08-27-2001||04:55 PM||||I see police chase videos on t.v. quite frequently.  Many of them go on for hours(one chase).  In the end the majority of the time the chase is haulted by use of a spike strip.  So why do they not use this option off of the get go.  I realize it takes a bit of time for an officer to get into postion to lay the strip, but hours...?  Come on.  Obviously the longer the chase goes on, the greater the potential for something to go wrong.  So why wait?  <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/side_cut_sig_4.jpg">  <BR>If you can read this, I'm parked.||||reg||1
